	doi = {10.3724/2096-7004.di.2025.0053},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105012989678&doi=10.3724%2F2096-7004.di.2025.0053&partnerID=40&md5=5cfa06f8909d5ef7b83912b084e3ca74},
	affiliations = {Maseno University, Department of Computer Science, Maseno, Nyanza, Kenya},
	abstract = {The growth of online platforms coupled with users’ ability to post and comment anonymously has resulted in an alarming spread of offensive and hateful content especially during election period. Efforts to monitor and detect hate speech, including computer based automated hate speech detection models remain unsatisfactory. This is primarily due to the fact that most annotated hate speech datasets focus on English, despite the growing use of low-resourced languages like Swahili and code-switched EnglishSwahili on Kenyan social media platforms such as Twitter (now X). Currently, there is only one annotated publicly available hate speech dataset having Swahili and code-switched English-Swahili texts. However, the dataset lacks language annotation distinguishing Swahili from English-Swahili. This poses a challenge, as the dataset also contains pure English tweets, making it difficult for researchers who require tweets in a specific language. Moreover, the dataset lacks hate target annotations, such as politics, disability, religion, ethnicity, social-status, nationality, gender, and others. This is a challenge since hate target labels help in comprehensive analysis of the hate stereotypes. This study therefore fills this gap by curating Swahili and code-switched English-Swahili hate speech dataset and annotating with hate class, target and language. Data preparation involved combining the Politikweli, AfriSenti, and Hate_Speech_Kenya datasets, resulting in a collection of 101,014 tweets. Hate target labels include nationality, social status, politics, disability, ethnicity, gender, religion and other. Multilingual index of 0.72 was achieved for code switching. Randolph’s free marginal kappa score of 0.435 and 0.552 was achieved for the first and second pilot dataset respectively with a significant difference of 0.117. The overall inter annotator score using Randolph’s multirater kappa was 0.70 showing substantial agreement. As a result, this study provides hate speech textual dataset having a total of 19369 tweets labeled as hate, offensive and neither. To assess the dataset’s suitability, experiments were conducted using a non-linear SVM and a Bi-directional Long Short-Term Memory (Bi-LSTM) model. SwahBERT model, a variant of BERT pre-trained on Swahili texts was fine-tuned on the code-switched data and achieved an F1-score of 0.89. Bi-LSTM model with fastText embeddings achieved an f1-score, accuracy, recall and precision of 0.8 on the test set showing the ability of fastText embeddings in capturing the nuances in code-switched texts. The recall for the hate speech class improved from 0.57 to 0.76 when the Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE) was applied, demonstrating the dataset’s practical applicability. Results for SVM trained on code-switched part of the dataset shows that traditional models performs better on a relatively smaller dataset. These results suggest that the dataset could be reused to develop machine learning models for moderation of bias and hate speech contents from social media especially in low-resource languages. This can help in promoting good mental health among social media users. The dataset can also serve as a benchmark for evaluating NLP techniques in low-resource settings. © 2025 Chinese Academy of Sciences.},
	author_keywords = {Data preprocessing; Dataset size; High resourced; Labels; Offensive},
	keywords = {Codes (symbols); Electronic voting; Social networking (online); Speech analysis; Speech coding; Speech communication; Tweets; Bi-directional; Data preprocessing; Data set size; High resourced; Memory modeling; Offensive; Short term memory; Social status; Speech detection; Target labels; Labeling},
