	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105025020537&doi=10.1109%2FICoEIT63558.2025.11211844&partnerID=40&md5=f436f69c9d20313009baa7fb05978a3d},
	affiliations = {Amity University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Noida, UP, India},
	abstract = {Online platforms are severely hampered by toxic remarks, which have a detrimental effect on user experience and public health. Using deep learning and natural language processing (NLP) techniques, this study offers a thorough method for detecting and categorising hazardous remarks. In order to achieve robust classification, we investigate a variety of deep learning models, such as transformer-based topologies, recurrent neural networks (RNN), and convolutional neural networks (CNN), using a dataset of labelled comments. To ensure optimal data representation, the study entails a number of preprocessing stages, such as tokenisation, lemmatisation, and addressing class imbalance. According to experimental findings, ensemble models that incorporate many architectures enhance classification accuracy by taking into account linguistic and contextual subtleties. We also evaluate the fairness of the model, talking about demographic biases and putting mitigation measures in place. The results underline the necessity of ongoing model improvement to accommodate changing harmful language trends and show the promise of deep learning and natural language processing in fostering safer online spaces. This study offers insightful information for enhancing automatic moderation systems and encouraging polite and productive online conversations. © 2025 IEEE.},
