	affiliations = {Newcastle University, School of Computing, Newcastle, Tyne and Wear, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {This study investigates how advanced large language models can be effectively leveraged to classify offensive content within severely imbalanced online textual data. We establish a standardized evaluation framework on the instruction-tuned Gemma-2-9b-it model to systematically assess Prompt engineering, Chain-of-Thought reasoning, and Low-Rank Adaptation fine-tuning in the context of imbalanced offensive language detection. Prompt engineering leverages pre-existing model knowledge, enabling lightweight zero-shot inference without additional training. CoT reasoning enhances interpretability by explicitly generating intermediate reasoning steps, thereby improving nuanced offensive language detection. LoRA fine-tuning adapts a minimal set of parameters, effectively addressing class imbalance while maintaining the model's general language understanding capabilities. Experimental evaluation on a manually annotated dataset of 5,948 chat messages categorized into Sexism, Sexual Harassment, Violence, Rude, and Other demonstrates that our proposed LoRA-based framework significantly surpasses both prompt-based and CoT approaches. Our method thus provides an efficient, interpretable, and robust solution for real-world offensive content moderation. © 2025 IEEE.},
