	year = {2025},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ASSIC64892.2025.11158467},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105018111847&doi=10.1109%2FASSIC64892.2025.11158467&partnerID=40&md5=7e138cf93d68539d1e972b711e97b494},
	affiliations = {MLR Institute of Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Hyderabad, TS, India},
	abstract = {Hate speech detection over the entire world wide web has become an extremely hot issue, since the era of social media. This paper presents a novel fusion approach to handle the classification task of hate speech from different content modalities, including text, memes, and image, through the aid of deep learning. The proposed system integrates Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) for object imaging and Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT) for text processing in order to achieve deep learning regarding the linguistic novelty and visual relationships. Leveraging a curated dataset from Instagram, the model is trained to discern subtle indicators of hate speech, employing advanced natural language processing techniques. Experimental results indicate significant improvements in the detection accuracy, precision, recall, and Fl-score, compared with the standard. The proposed approach not only enhances the robustness of hate speech detection but also provides the functionalities of real-time intervention and can thus provide a scalable solution for online platforms to prevent the propagation of harmful information. In this work, the question of how and to what extent hate speech impacts, and can be automated is discussed, and this opens the way for further implementations of automated content moderation for hate speech. © 2025 IEEE.},
