	abstract = {The increasing prevalence of hate speech on digital platforms has generated considerable interest in automated methodologies to identify harmful and abusive content. This paper introduces a Multimodal hate speech detection system specifically designed for Dravidian languages, focusing on Malayalam, Tamil, and Telugu. The proposed system integrates both textual and acoustic features to achieve a more robust and nuanced understanding of hate speech. Mel-Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCCs) are extracted from the audio data, while textual data is processed using Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF). The deep learning model employs Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks to process text and a dense neural network for audio features; their outputs are combined in a joint classification layer. The performance of the system is evaluated on datasets in Malayalam, Tamil, and Telugu demonstrating promising results with high accuracy and potential for real-world application. These findings suggest that multimodal approaches enhance the detection of hate speech, particularly in languages characterized by complex linguistic and acoustic features. © 2025 IEEE.},
