	affiliations = {Universitas Gadjah Mada, Department of Electronics and Computer Science, Yogyakarta, Yogyakarta, Indonesia; Universitas Muhammadiyah Surakarta, Department of Informatics, Surakarta, Central Java, Indonesia},
	abstract = {The growth of social media has created new communication patterns in Indonesia, where code-mixing between Indonesian and regional languages such as Javanese and Sundanese is increasingly common. This phenomenon presents significant challenges in automated content moderation, especially in detecting hate speech. This study aims to detect hate speech in Javanese-Indonesian and Sundanese-Indonesian mixed language texts. The main challenge in this dataset is class imbalance, which can affect the performance of the classification model. To overcome these challenges, this research explores various machine learning approaches, including traditional models (Random Forest, Multinomial Naïve Bayes, Support Vector Machine, Decision Tree, and Logistic Regression), Recurrent Neural Network models (LSTM and BiLSTM), and Transformer-based models (XLM-Roberta, DistilBERT Multilingual, IndoBERT, and DistilBERT Indonesian). In addition, sampling methods such as Random Oversampling and Random Undersampling are applied to balance the class distribution in the dataset. The results show that the Transformer-based method provides the best performance in detecting hate speech on code-mixed datasets. The application of oversampling techniques is shown to improve the detection of minority classes without causing significant overfitting. Thus, this research contributes to the development of a more adaptive and contextualized hate speech detection system for multilingual environments in Indonesia, and offers insights for further research in natural language processing (NLP) for low-resource local languages. © 2025 IEEE.},
